{:edb/db-before-t 1598 :edb/db-after-t 1599 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534911 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:41:49.780Z" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186118095 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118099 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119078 1078 #edb/ref 17592186119080 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443896 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443897 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443901 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443902 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443899 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443898 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443900 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186119750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186443903 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120033 1050 1020.0 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120326 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩,\\,0\\le s<2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120326 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n$r(s) = ⟨\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}, 3\\cos (\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}), 3\\sin (\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩, 0 \\le s < 2\\sqrt{10}π$." 13194139534911 17592186045417 false] [17592186120326 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨0,\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{0+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{10}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120494 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s$. Find the curvature at the point where $s = \\frac{3π}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n$T(s) = ⟨ - \\sin \\frac{s}{3}, \\cos \\frac{s}{3}, 0⟩$." 13194139534911 17592186045417 false] [17592186120494 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{3},\\,0⟩.\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186120494 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{3},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186121002 1064 "The **curvature** of a curve measures how much the curve \"bends\" at a given point. It can also be thought of as a measure of how much the curve deviates from being a straight line. Curvature is denoted by the Greek letter $\\kappa$ (pronounced \"kappa\"). \n\nIf $\\kappa=0$ at some point on a curve, then that curve looks like a straight line in some neighborhood of the point.\n\nIf the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ is parametrized by the arc length $s,$ then the curvature $\\kappa$ is the magnitude of the change of direction of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T$ per unit of arc length. That is,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|= \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|.\n$$\n\nNote that $|| \\mathbf v ||$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(s)= \\left\\langle \\sin\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}, \\: -\\cos\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \nwhich is parametrized by arc length $s.$ Let's find the curvature of  $\\mathbf r(s)$  at the point where $s={\\pi}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) =\\dfrac 12 \\left\\langle \\cos\\left( \\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\sqrt{3}, \\: \\sin \\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate $\\mathbf{T}(s)$ and evaluate its absolute value at $s=\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{s}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{s}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{'}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature by taking the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{'}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** To calculate $\\kappa,$ the function $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ should be at least twice differentiable with respect to $s.$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 false] [17592186121002 1064 "The **curvature** of a curve measures how much the curve \"bends\" at a given point. It can also be thought of as a measure of how much the curve deviates from being a straight line. Curvature is denoted by the Greek letter $\\kappa$ (pronounced \"kappa\"). \n\nIf $\\kappa=0$ at some point on a curve, then that curve looks like a straight line in some neighborhood of the point.\n\nIf the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ is parametrized by the arc length $s,$ then the curvature $\\kappa$ is the magnitude of the change of direction of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T$ per unit of arc length. That is,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|= \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|.\n$$\n\nNote that $|| \\mathbf v ||$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(s)= \\left\\langle \\sin\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}, \\: -\\cos\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \nwhich is parametrized by arc length $s.$ Let's find the curvature of  $\\mathbf r(s)$  at the point where $s={\\pi}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) =\\dfrac 12 \\left\\langle \\cos\\left( \\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\sqrt{3}, \\: \\sin \\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate $\\mathbf{T}(s)$ and evaluate its absolute value at $s=\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature by taking the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** To calculate $\\kappa,$ the function $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ should be at least twice differentiable with respect to $s.$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186121007 1064 "If the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is, remember that the arc length $s$ and the parameter $t$ are related by the formula \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle s(t)= \\int_0^t\\left\\| \\mathbf  r'(x)\\right\\|\\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nBy the chain rule and the fundamental theorem of calculus, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\dfrac{\\textrm d  s}{\\textrm d t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}‖ & =‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}s}‖‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =κ(t)‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ \\\\ κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{'}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 false] [17592186121007 1064 "If the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is, remember that the arc length $s$ and the parameter $t$ are related by the formula \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle s(t)= \\int_0^t\\left\\| \\mathbf  r'(x)\\right\\|\\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nBy the chain rule and the fundamental theorem of calculus, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\dfrac{\\textrm d  s}{\\textrm d t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}‖ & =‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}s}‖‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =κ(t)‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ \\\\ κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443848 1080 "\\frac{2}{25}" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443848 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443848 1083 #uuid "03623963-16d7-553e-92b9-9f3efa2090ad" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443849 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443849 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443849 1083 #uuid "0b5588c5-37db-5b02-87d7-05e19f211482" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443850 1080 "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443850 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443850 1083 #uuid "12432056-a810-519c-9825-13b25ab2a228" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443851 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443851 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443851 1083 #uuid "1f54c52e-665b-579e-86df-670272b9e806" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443852 1080 "1" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443852 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443852 1083 #uuid "206b9917-9249-5d96-885f-9052cced1472" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443853 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443853 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443853 1083 #uuid "2be96d5c-0914-5fe5-acba-da0c7e6d8b3a" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443854 1080 "\\frac{1}{4\\pi }" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443854 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443854 1083 #uuid "2d492172-299d-5f9e-8553-a991858c87c0" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443855 1080 "3" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443855 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443855 1083 #uuid "3260ca78-14a6-50d0-b36b-a