 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443891 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1069 "q-131742" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s={\\pi }^{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1072 #uuid "4e6f234b-737b-5376-aaeb-5d730bef9b84" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443896 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s={\\pi }^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}({\\pi }^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ({\\pi }^{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}({\\pi }^{2})‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443895 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1069 "q-131743" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩.\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1072 #uuid "84ec701f-8d6f-546b-8f54-4258332a1413" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443897 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-1⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ \\\\  & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443890 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1069 "q-83657" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}),\\,3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})⟩,\\,0\\le s<8\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1072 #uuid "8e84a8d6-a1ed-5f78-832b-753ef57840b9" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443898 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{4}|\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{4}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443894 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1069 "q-83663" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\,-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3})i+12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,j,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1072 #uuid "941975f4-ea50-5edb-a299-e8568f71e188" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443899 1074 "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3}))}^{2}+{(12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12{t}^{2})^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})+(12{t}^{2})^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12{t}^{2})^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡({t}^{3}))} \\\\  & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3}))}^{2}+{(-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3{t}^{2})^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})+(-3{t}^{2})^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2})^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡({t}^{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡({t}^{3}))} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{12{t}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443892 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1069 "q-83643" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where:\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{3-8s}{10},\\,\\frac{6s+4}{10}⟩,\\,s\\ge 0\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1072 #uuid "a4a4a281-bd0c-502e-a09a-cc4e77ac53a7" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443900 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3-8s}{10}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+4}{10})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{10},\\,\\frac{6}{10}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443888 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1069 "q-80521" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=2{\\pi }^{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n \n\n$$\nT(s)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1072 #uuid "c4f686a5-889f-5e90-8c7e-36ce4fec4b92" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443901 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=2{\\pi }^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}}{\\sqrt{2s}}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}}{\\sqrt{2s}}\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(2{\\pi }^{2}) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2(2{\\pi }^{2})}}{\\sqrt{2(2{\\pi }^{2})}}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2(2{\\pi }^{2})}}{\\sqrt{2(2{\\pi }^{2})}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2{\\pi }^{2}) & =‖{T}^{′}(2{\\pi }^{2})‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\pi })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443893 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1069 "q-131732" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=0$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,3⟩.\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1072 #uuid "d152b171-fd28-5ce0-9099-77a199871a6f" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443902 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{25}⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443889 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1069 "q-131945" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\frac{4}{5}s⟩,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1072 #uuid "d96a6265-af2d-5821-9fbc-e9e0d0216e3e" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true] [17592186443903 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{5}s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{25}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{5}),\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{3}{25}|\\cdot \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{5})+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534911 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03623963-16d7-553e-92b9-9f3efa2090ad" 17592186443848 "answer-0b5588c5-37db-5b02-87d7-05e19f211482" 17592186443849 "answer-12432056-a810-519c-9825-13b25ab2a228" 17592186443850 "answer-1f54c52e-665b-579e-86df-670272b9e806" 17592186443851 "answer-206b9917-9249-5d96-885f-9052cced1472" 17592186443852 "answer-2be96d5c-0914-5fe5-acba-da0c7e6d8b3a" 17592186443853 "answer-2d492172-299d-5f9e-8553-a991858c87c0" 17592186443854 "answer-3260ca78-14a6-50d0-b36b-a07da635b595" 17592186443855 "answer-38a62d49-ab80-5de1-b7d6-df3c195fadb0" 17592186443856 "answer-3c9b736d-97b9-5346-ae4c-3f48e12a4b99" 17592186443857 "answer-4efce17f-3075-5e92-ad94-a11200baf0b2" 17592186443858 "answer-50a64c5e-ee2e-5e33-99cd-2507a3e15ed4" 17592186443859 "answer-5562ffb